Fix AVX2 end-of-data false negative in the double-shufti accelerator.

run_accel() passed `c_end - 1` to shuftiDoubleExec(), stopping one byte early. The
double-shufti character-class accelerator then over-skipped near the end of the
buffer, so block scans (and streaming chunks) SILENTLY MISSED matches ending at the
last byte -- e.g. `\db+` / `[0-9]b+` / `[a-c]+a.?` / `[a-c]+$` on inputs >= ~18 bytes.
Only the AVX2 build is affected (the default SSE4.2 build is correct); reproduces on
both clang-cl (MSVC) and gcc (Linux), so it is an upstream Vectorscan bug, present in
5.4.11 AND 5.4.12, fixed only on `develop`.

This backports the caller fix from upstream commit
de317291e9c46f8060719f655920043fabc968ed ("shufti-double: fix regressions from #325",
PR #368). Verified (Docker, gcc, AVX2): restores the missed matches with no false
positives. 5.4.11 uses the pre-SuperVector shufti, so only the one-line caller change
is needed (develop's accompanying check_last_byte / first_char_mask tweaks are for the
newer SuperVector implementation and do not apply here).

diff --git a/src/nfa/accel.c b/src/nfa/accel.c
index 7661b7a7..c00f4993 100644
--- a/src/nfa/accel.c
+++ b/src/nfa/accel.c
@@ -151,11 +151,14 @@ const u8 *run_accel(const union AccelAux *accel, const u8 *c, const u8 *c_end) {
             return c;
         }
 
-        /* need to stop one early to get an accurate end state */
+        /* Backport of upstream de317291 (PR #368): pass c_end, not c_end - 1.
+         * Stopping one byte early made shufti-double (the character-class accelerator)
+         * miss matches ending at the last byte of a buffer/chunk (an AVX2 end-of-data
+         * false negative). shuftiDoubleExec handles the final byte correctly itself. */
         rv = shuftiDoubleExec(accel->dshufti.lo1,
                               accel->dshufti.hi1,
                               accel->dshufti.lo2,
-                              accel->dshufti.hi2, c, c_end - 1);
+                              accel->dshufti.hi2, c, c_end);
         break;
 
     case ACCEL_RED_TAPE:
